        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "Predicting what comes next"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "1, 3, 6, 10, 15 ... can you guess the next number? Spotting the rule behind a list lets you predict any term without writing them all."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "AP: add d",
          "GP: multiply by r",
          "explicit and recursive",
          "sequence, term",
          "t_n = a + (n - 1)d",
          "t_n = a r^(n - 1)",
          "t_n = n(n + 1)/2",
          "test: difference or ratio"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "A sequence is an ordered list of numbers, and each number is called a term.\nAn explicit formula gives a term from its position n. A recursive formula gives a term from the previous terms.\nThe triangular numbers 1, 3, 6, 10, 15 have nth term n(n + 1)/2, which is also the sum of the first n natural numbers.\nIn an arithmetic progression each term is obtained by adding a fixed number d, the common difference.\nThe nth term of an AP is t_n = a + (n - 1)d, where a is the first term.\nIn a geometric progression each term is obtained by multiplying by a fixed number r, the common ratio.\nThe nth term of a GP is t_n = a r^(n - 1). For 2, 6, 18 the ratio is 3.\nCheck which kind it is: subtract consecutive terms for an AP, divide them for a GP."
        }
      },
